  "requirement": "The review prompt's criteria count as compound a requirement that lists several\nindependent forms, checks, or outputs. An additional promise introduced with\n\"also\" is a hint, regardless of whether the text uses RFC keywords. The reviewer\nsplits independent obligations that could be implemented, change, or fail on\ntheir own, and moves shared detail, such as a template or format, into its own\nrequirement that the others cite.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The requirement text prescribes no libraries, algorithms, signatures, types, fields or delegation through named interfaces. Every clause describes what the review prompt's criteria say and how a reviewer should behave. I checked each clause and found no structural obligation.\n\n- \"count as compound a requirement that lists several independent forms, checks, or outputs\".\n- \"An additional promise introduced with 'also' is a hint, regardless of whether the text uses RFC keywords\".\n- \"splits independent obligations that could be implemented, change, or fail on their own\".\n- \"moves shared detail, such as a template or format, into its own requirement that the others cite\".\n\nThe review prompt could be organised differently and still meet all four clauses. It could place the Compound criterion in another section or word it differently. These choices are discretionary, so no clause constrains how the prompt is built.\n\nThe supplied root is `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/references/review-prompt.md`. Its `section[Requirement group review]/section[Criteria]` section holds a **Compound** paragraph that states each of these points. The paragraph covers independent forms, checks and outputs, and the \"also\" hint regardless of RFC keywords. It also gives the test \"could one part be implemented, change, or fail while another holds? Then split it\". It tells the reviewer to move shared detail, such as a template or format, into its own requirement that the others cite. So the root agrees with the text.\n\nThe prompt's coverage of the clauses is behavioral evidence of what the prompt says. It is not a structural obligation, and no further implementation root is needed.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement governs the content of the review prompt's criteria. It has four obligations:\n1. A requirement that lists several independent forms, checks, or outputs counts as compound.\n2. An additional promise introduced with \"also\" is a hint, whether or not the text uses RFC keywords.\n3. The reviewer splits independent obligations that could be implemented, change, or fail on their own.\n4. The reviewer moves shared detail, such as a template or format, into its own requirement that the others cite.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_review_prompt_splits_compound_requirements]` is marked `{test}` for the subject. It reads the `## Criteria` section of the review prompt through `review_prompt()` and `section(...)`. `review_prompt()` loads `references/review-prompt.md`. `section` returns the section body with whitespace collapsed, so phrases that wrap across lines still match. `assert_text` then asserts that the section contains four phrases:\n- the Compound definition (\"a requirement that lists several independent forms, checks, or outputs\"), for obligation 1;\n- the \"also\" hint \"regardless of whether the text uses RFC keywords\", for obligation 2;\n- the split test \"could one part be implemented, change, or fail while another holds? Then split it\", for obligation 3;\n- \"Move shared detail, such as a template or a format, into its own requirement that the others cite\", for obligation 4.\n\nI read the prompt's Criteria paragraph, and it contains all four phrases. A prompt that dropped any of the four would fail the test, so the assertions enforce the obligations. The prompt text is the only observable behavior here, and the test checks it. No scenario is missing, and no marking gap exists.",
